record(s,t,rows,decs,inv,'The page gives two formulations of the Hodge data associated with a place, a scalar extension and a filtered space. The first formulation explicitly writes Hξ(W)^(p), with W, although the opening module is V; the ink is retained and no V is substituted. The upper weight note reads p and −2, with the word norme still uncertain and the article le unchanged. The second opening has C-module, as shown by the doubled left stroke shared with the C field letters. The real-place paragraph names frobξ, then its primed semilinear extension, and asks for identity on Hξ(V); the written “½-linéaire” is preserved. The overwritten initial G exponent is not settled from the later G⁰ occurrences. The margin field diagram has final index ∞. These are reports of the page, without an independent proof of the constructions or compatibility claim. Archive number 164.',doubts=doubts,notation=[['Filtration indices',r'H_\xi(W)^{(p)}, H_\xi(V)^{(p)}, H_\xi(V)^p','165','W occurs once visibly; parentheses vary as written.'],['Semilinear extension',r"\operatorname{frob}_\xi,\operatorname{frob}'_\xi; \frac12\text{-linéaire}",'165','Prime and numerical half are visible.'],['Field decoration',r'\widetilde K; \overline K_\xi','165','Inserted upper field has a wave, lower display has a straight bar.']],hand=['The C field letter has a doubled vertical stroke inside a broad open curve. This supports C at the lower module opening, against C’s G|C and H’s E. Other G letters on the page have their separate inward/lower spur.','The overwritten superscript on the opening G remains uncertain despite clear later G⁰ forms. A repeated mathematical role does not restore covered strokes.'],abbreviations=[['coh.','cohomologie'],['N.B.','nota bene'],['½-linéaire','semi-linéaire']],extra='Thirteen crops were used. The sole crop used only for a retained cancellation doubt is scan 165 (1529,1451,1640,1526), cancelled le. The weight-letter crop is scan 165 (897,18,959,83); the other eleven appear in the evidence rows. No diagram arrowheads were added to the four plain field-connection strokes.')
